{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>You have begun</b> Two times in the Masorah: אתה החלות, אשר החלות. — You have begun to let fall before Him: to show what You conquered before Sihon and Og. — And this החלות לנפול לפניו: to show what He began to let fall. תחנונים ידבר רש ועשיר יענה עזות — ס\"ת רמוז משה. And the verse begins with tav and ends with tav, because he did not merit to enter the land, which is ת' paras by ת' paras." "<b>to show</b> Tagin on the resh — You have shown me even what I did not ask of You." "<b>to show</b> Three times in the Masorah, and the others: להראות העמים והשרים את יפיה; אשר נתן בשושן להשמידם — He gave him להראות את אסתר. This is what they expounded: The Holy One, blessed be He, showed Moses everything that would befall Israel — how they would dwell in their tranquility and all the oppressors destined to oppress them. And this is להראות העמים והשרים את יפיה — how they are in their beauty and their tranquility. And likewise להשמידם נתן לו להראות — He showed him the oppressors against them." "<b>Your servant</b> — as You said, כן עבדי משה. The verse begins with kaf-alef and ends with kaf, and it has 21 words, corresponding to אך טוב לישראל and corresponding to אהי'."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The three Masorah citations appear to be: אתה החלות (Deut 3:24), אשר החלות (Num 21:11 or similar), and לנפול לפניו (uncertain). The phrase תחנונים ידבר רש ועשיר יענה עזות appears to be a proverbial or midrashic citation; its source is uncertain. The expansion of ס\"ת and the phrase ת' פרסה על ת' פרסה (400 parasang by 400 parasang, a rabbinic measure of the Land of Israel) are rendered as written.", 2 "The two other Masorah citations are identified as from the book of Esther: להראות העמים והשרים את יפיה (Esth 1:11) and אשר נתן בשושן להשמידם (Esth 9:25, abbreviated). The phrase להראות את אסתר does not appear verbatim in Esther; it may be a midrashic paraphrase.", 3 "The verse begins with החלות (heh) in the biblical text, not kaf-alef; Ba'al HaTurim may be referring to a different demarcation or reading. אך טוב לישראל (Ps 73:1) and אהי' (=21, the gematria of the divine name אהיה) both equal 21. The claim of 21 words in Deut 3:24 is preserved as written."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:08:50.483930902Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}